<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Codex Mendoza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1541</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Codex Mendoza is an Aztec pictorial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> produced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> around 1541, probably for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-mendoza">Antonio de Mendoza</a>, with Spanish glosses and commentary. It records the reigns and conquests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> rulers, the tribute owed by each province of the empire, and scenes of daily life from birth to old age. It is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodleian-library">Bodleian Library</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, and is a chief source for Aztec tribute and society.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/codex-mendoza.jpg" alt="Codex Mendoza" width="320" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CodexMendoza01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
